<div class="applet-shell">
  <div class="applet-titlebar">
    <span class="applet-title">Getting Started &mdash; A Walkthrough</span>
  </div>

  <div style="padding: 16px 20px; max-width: 900px;">

    <p class="admin-help">
      This is a plain-language walkthrough of actually using this app, start to finish &mdash; setting up your
      first landlord, tenant, and invoice, and recording your first payment. If you're looking for how the
      <em>formula language</em> works (for building your own calculated fields or reports), that's a separate
      page: <a href="/admin/help">Formula Language Reference</a>. This page is about the everyday workflow, not
      the technical building blocks underneath it.
    </p>

    <div class="applet-titlebar applet-titlebar-child"><span class="applet-title">The Big Picture</span></div>
    <p class="admin-help">
      Everything in this app connects together in one natural chain:
    </p>
    <p class="admin-help" style="font-family:monospace; background:#f7f6f0; padding:10px 14px; border:1px solid var(--border-dark);">
      Landlord &rarr; Property &rarr; Tenant &rarr; Invoice &rarr; Payment
    </p>
    <p class="admin-help">
      A <strong>Landlord</strong> owns one or more <strong>Properties</strong>. Each Property has one or more
      <strong>Tenants</strong> renting space in it. Each Tenant gets billed periodically via an
      <strong>Invoice</strong>. Money coming in against those invoices, and money going out to people you pay
      (contractors, staff, anyone), gets tracked as a <strong>Payment</strong>. Setting things up roughly follows
      that same order &mdash; you can't create a Tenant until the Property and Landlord it belongs to already
      exist, for instance.
    </p>

    <div class="applet-titlebar applet-titlebar-child"><span class="applet-title">Step 1 &mdash; Add a Landlord</span></div>
    <p class="admin-help">
      Go to the <strong>Landlords</strong> tab and click <strong>+ New Record</strong>. Fill in their identity
      details &mdash; name, PAN, GSTIN, address, and so on. If a landlord operates in more than one state (and
      is GST-registered separately in each), you'll see a <strong>Group</strong> field &mdash; leave that pointing
      at itself for the very first registration you create for this landlord (more on this in Step 4, since the
      same idea applies to Tenants too, and it's easier to explain with a concrete example there).
    </p>

    <div class="applet-titlebar applet-titlebar-child"><span class="applet-title">Step 2 &mdash; Add a Property</span></div>
    <p class="admin-help">
      Go to <strong>Property</strong>, click <strong>+ New Record</strong>, and link it to the Landlord you just
      created. Fill in the address and, importantly, the <strong>State</strong> the property is actually located
      in &mdash; this matters for GST later (it decides whether a bill is CGST+SGST or something else).
    </p>

    <div class="applet-titlebar applet-titlebar-child"><span class="applet-title">Step 3 &mdash; Add Banking Info</span></div>
    <p class="admin-help">
      If you're tracking security deposits or other banking details tied to a tenant, set those up under
      <strong>Banking</strong> before creating the tenant itself, since the Tenant form will ask you to link to
      an existing Banking record rather than create one on the fly.
    </p>

    <div class="applet-titlebar applet-titlebar-child"><span class="applet-title">Step 4 &mdash; Add a Tenant (and understanding revisions)</span></div>
    <p class="admin-help">
      Go to <strong>Tenants</strong> and create your first tenant, linking them to the Property they're renting.
      Give them a distinctive <strong>Tenant Code</strong> &mdash; something like <code>tenantA_1</code>, where
      the trailing <code>_1</code> means "first version of this tenant's terms."
    </p>
    <p class="admin-help">
      <strong>Why "first version"?</strong> Rent and lease terms change over time &mdash; a renewal, a rate
      increase, a change in service category. If you just edited the same tenant record every time that
      happened, every <em>old</em> invoice tied to that tenant would suddenly look like it used the
      <em>new</em> terms too, which isn't right &mdash; an invoice from last year should always reflect what was
      actually true last year. So instead, a change in terms means creating a <strong>new</strong> tenant record
      (e.g. <code>tenantA_2</code>) rather than editing the old one.
    </p>
    <p class="admin-help">
      To keep all these revisions recognizably "the same underlying tenant," you'll see a <strong>Group</strong>
      field on the Tenant form. For the very first revision, set this to point at <em>itself</em> &mdash; you'll
      need to save the record once first, then edit it again and pick itself from the dropdown (it can't appear
      in its own list until it already exists). For every <em>later</em> revision of that same tenant, set Group
      to point at that same first record. There's also an <strong>Is Current</strong> checkbox &mdash; exactly
      one revision in the group should have this checked at any time (the active one); when you create a new
      revision, remember to uncheck it on the old one.
    </p>
    <p class="admin-help">
      This sounds like more steps than it is in practice &mdash; you'll only touch this when creating a
      genuinely new revision, which for most tenants might be once a year or less.
    </p>

    <div class="applet-titlebar applet-titlebar-child"><span class="applet-title">Step 5 &mdash; Create an Invoice</span></div>
    <p class="admin-help">
      Go to <strong>Invoices</strong> and create one, linking it to the Tenant being billed and the Invoice Date.
      Most of the other fields &mdash; base rent, CGST/SGST percentages and amounts, the total &mdash; fill
      themselves in automatically once you save, calculated from the tenant's terms and the current GST rate for
      that month. You shouldn't need to type those in by hand.
    </p>

    <div class="applet-titlebar applet-titlebar-child"><span class="applet-title">Step 6 &mdash; Record a Payment</span></div>
    <p class="admin-help">
      Go to <strong>Payments</strong> to record money going out to a Payee (set up under <strong>Payees</strong>
      first if you haven't already). If a Payee has a UPI ID on file, you'll see a QR code you can scan directly
      from your banking app to actually send the payment &mdash; no need to type an amount or UPI ID by hand into
      your bank's app.
    </p>

    <div class="applet-titlebar applet-titlebar-child"><span class="applet-title">Where to Go From Here</span></div>
    <p class="admin-help">
      That's the whole everyday loop. A few things worth knowing exist, once you're comfortable with the basics:
    </p>
    <ul class="admin-help" style="padding-left: 20px;">
      <li><strong>Reports</strong> &mdash; build your own custom views of the data (e.g. "every unpaid invoice this quarter") without needing anyone to build it for you. See Admin &rarr; Reports.</li>
      <li><strong>Formula fields</strong> &mdash; if you want to calculate something new (a new field that derives its value from others), see the <a href="/admin/help">Formula Language Reference</a> for the exact syntax available.</li>
      <li><strong>Backup</strong> &mdash; take a backup (Admin &rarr; Backup) before any big change you're unsure about. It's a safety net, and restoring an old one is straightforward if something goes wrong.</li>
    </ul>

  </div>
</div>

<%- include('../partials/foot') %>
